00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.890
So this is all possible
thanks to Kubernetes.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.880
So, as you know, Kubernetes is
the open source orchestration

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:38.690
layer for managing and
deploying containers.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:42.320
Announced only four years
ago, Kubernetes is now

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.830
supported by every major cloud.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.930
It has taken off like wildfire.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.990
Every day, thousands
of people contribute

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.530
to the success of Kubernetes,
allowing real businesses

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:59.760
like yours to abstract away
the underlying infrastructure.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.360
And, in fact, it's that power of
abstracting away the underlying

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.490
infrastructure that has allowed
us to think of infrastructure

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:13.350
as a smooth, paved surface,
available wherever you are,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.689
in your data center or in ours.

00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:16.980
But that's only half the story.

00:02:19.369 --> 00:02:21.410
RYAN GREGG: The other half
of the story for today

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.870
is serverless, which
is fundamentally

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:28.250
changing the way that developers
approach building applications.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.470
Some of you in the industry
may think of serverless

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.510
as only meaning functions.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.790
But there's so much more to
the promise of serverless

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:37.970
than just running snippets
of code in the cloud.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.570
In fact, we at Google see
serverless not just as compute,

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.240
but as the whole stack
that allows developers

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.780
to focus on what really matters,
that core nugget of business

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.770
value, and not have
to be concerned

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.770
with the complexities of
the infrastructure that

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.120
runs that code.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.210
In fact, Google has been
working on serverless

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:57.470
for more than a decade.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.590
10 years ago-- almost exactly--
was it April, I guess?--

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.840
we launched App Engine,
the very first platform

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.110
as a service, and the very first
Google cloud-- or, sorry, one

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.780
of the first platforms of
service, and the very first

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:10.190
Google Cloud product.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.940
Since that time, over
the last 10 years,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.730
we've continued to
innovate, providing

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.010
a number of additional
serverless solutions.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.720
And today we were
very happy to announce

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.615
the general availability
of Cloud Functions.

00:03:19.615 --> 00:03:21.298
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.910
That is super exciting.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.070
But there's so much
more to serverless.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.334
This is just a journey
that's still beginning.

00:03:31.334 --> 00:03:33.000
And I'd like to unpack
a little bit what

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.770
the promise of
serverless really is.

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.610
What do we see it as?

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.030
Now at Google we like
to joke that serverless

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:40.599
is about no servers.

00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:43.140
And, obviously, there are still
servers running a data center

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.550
somewhere.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.530
But it's about making
sure that developers

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:49.300
don't have to be concerned
with what those servers are,

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.090
what that infrastructure
that underlies

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.220
your application really is.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.774
If you want to bring a
container, deploy a function,

00:03:57.774 --> 00:03:59.190
or just an
application, you should

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.100
be able to do that regardless of
what the underlying platform is

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.690
that it will run on.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.300
It also means being idiomatic,
which is another Google-ism,

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.070
I think--

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:11.080
which is really to say that it's
native to you as a developer.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:13.330
It's meeting the
developers where they are.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.191
So if you're a Go
developer, you should just

00:04:15.191 --> 00:04:16.399
be able to be a Go developer.

00:04:16.399 --> 00:04:19.690
You shouldn't have to learn a
bunch of quirks in our system.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:21.540
If you want to be a
Node.js developer,

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:22.860
just be an Node.js developer.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.920
Don't worry about how to
integrate with the platform.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.442
And then the third promise
is about being event-driven.

00:04:28.442 --> 00:04:30.900
Today, if you want to wire up
a bunch of different systems,

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.900
you typically end up hard-coding
those systems together,

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:35.790
putting all of that
logic into your code,

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.950
instead of being able to
declaratively bind things

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.100
through in a venting framework.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:43.270
And we at Google see
one more promise here,

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.450
which is that serverless
really should be keeping

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.430
you free of vendor lock-in.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.380
By adopting the
promise of serverless,

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:50.940
you should be able
to take your code

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.760
and run it wherever you feel
most appropriate, if that's

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.180
an on-premises data center,
a third-party data center,

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:58.470
or in the cloud.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:04:59.700
You shouldn't have to choose.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.850
You should be able to
keep using the framework

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.340
that you're using.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.840
So Kubernetes and serverless,
if you think about it,

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.480
are kind of two sides of the
same pursuit, two things that

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.079
go great together, like
chocolate and peanut butter--

00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:15.370
unless you happen to be
allergic to chocolate or peanut

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:19.450
butter and then it's
absolutely nothing like that.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:22.339
But what Kubernetes
does for the operator,

00:05:22.339 --> 00:05:24.130
hiding away all of the
sort of complexities

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:26.350
of the underlying
infrastructure that you're

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:29.140
running on and allowing
the smooth, paved surface,

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.240
serverless does for
developers, allowing

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.880
them to not be concerned about
the platform they're running on

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.460
and just focus on the core
value of the code that matters.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:39.520
And, in fact, we at
Google believe in this

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.250
so strongly that this
is the future of the way

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:44.920
that compute should be
run, that this morning we

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:48.910
were happy to announce the GKE
serverless add-on, which brings

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:52.210
all of the wonderful operator
friendliness of Kubernetes

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.430
and all of the developer
friendliness of serverless

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:58.650
together into our
Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:02.180
So the benefits of using
the serverless add-on--

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:05.450
number one, it's easy to start
deploying with less code.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.112
Today, if you want to deploy
an application on Kubernetes,

00:06:08.112 --> 00:06:10.070
you have to deal with a
bunch of configuration.

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.170
You have to write all
of that and check it in.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:14.600
The serverless add-on makes
this much, much simpler.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.820
And I'll show you
how in a little bit.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:20.150
It also makes it easy to run
a serverless-style workload,

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.210
being able to go from source all
the way to a deployed container

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:25.631
with a URL in minutes.

00:06:25.631 --> 00:06:28.130
It automatically deals with all
the network programming, all

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.830
of the provisioning ingress,
and all of the different layers

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:34.450
inside Kubernetes that you used
to have to deal with before.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:36.430
And it helps auto-scaling.

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:38.050
And you might be
telling yourself,

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:39.846
hey, Kubernetes
already auto-scales.

00:06:39.846 --> 00:06:41.470
Why is he talking
about this in context

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:42.745
of the serverless add-on?

00:06:42.745 --> 00:06:45.160
And you're right--
Kubernetes does scale.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:47.410
But the serverless add-on
makes it scale better.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:50.080
And it makes it scale in a way
that Kubernetes doesn't today.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:51.640
We can actually
scale your workload

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.690
all the way down
to 0, which lets

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:57.460
an operator in your company
pack more applications

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:00.550
into the same cluster resources
and have everything continue

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:03.110
to run really well.

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:06.600
So, now that we've introduced
the serverless add-on,

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.255
let's actually show
it to you in action.

00:07:10.255 --> 00:07:13.550
If we can switch over to my
laptop, I'll be very happy.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:14.601
Good.

00:07:14.601 --> 00:07:16.350
So the very first thing
that we need to do

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:19.800
is stand up a cluster with the
serverless add-on installed.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.800
And this is an alpha that
we're talking about today.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.230
So you'll see I'm using the
alpha parameter in G Cloud.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.390
But we're just going to
go create a new cluster

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.760
and enable the
serverless add-on.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.180
And, you know, let's
see if this works.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:34.917
Aha, there we go.

00:07:34.917 --> 00:07:36.750
So I got a couple of
warnings about defaults

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:37.499
that are changing.

00:07:37.499 --> 00:07:38.490
But that's fine.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:40.770
Meanwhile, I'm going to
go switch over and use

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:42.853
a cluster that already
exists so that I don't have

00:07:42.853 --> 00:07:44.530
to sit here and watch
that little thing

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:46.210
spin for a second or two.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:48.210
I always like to get
started with a new platform

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.520
