There is just my
Hello World sample.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:48.780
I'm going to dump the
YAML real quick just

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:50.780
to show you that there's
actually YAML behind it

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:52.290
and point out a couple things.

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:53.790
There's a bunch of
status conditions

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:55.929
on how the service has changed.

00:10:55.929 --> 00:10:57.970
But, simply, here's the
spec that it got created.

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:00.180
So it defined a
concurrency model for me,

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:02.610
that my container supports
multi-concurrency.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.890
And it just points at
the container image--

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:06.330
really, really simple thing.

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.300
And it has the domain for
where I'm being served from

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.040
and the revision.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.090
So what's a revision?

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:16.680
Well, the revision is actually
another one of the CRDs

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.090
that we published.

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.560
And revision is the immutable
state of my application.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.610
So it's tying all of the
configuration details

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:25.650
and the actual
container image together

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:28.350
into an object inside
Kubernetes that describes it.

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:29.880
And, as I continue
to make changes,

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.130
it continues to
snap new revisions.

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:34.230
So it's easy to roll
back or to split traffic

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:36.510
between multiple
revisions or deal

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:39.965
with these sort of multiple
deployments that you want to.

00:11:39.965 --> 00:11:41.340
And then the last
object that I'm

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:44.270
going to show you is the route.

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:47.540
And what the route does is it is
another CRD that just connects

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:50.150
between the outside world
and the revision that's

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:51.810
running inside my container.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:53.840
In this case, it's
using HTTP to route

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:56.000
from that Hello World
domain that was published

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.410
to the particular revision.

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:00.326
And it's always pointing at
the latest revision by default.

00:12:00.326 --> 00:12:02.450
But if I wanted to point
out a particular revision,

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:04.830
I could do that as well.

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:05.330
All right.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:09.220
Let's do a little bit
more advanced sample.

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:13.330
So this time around, I have
written an actual guestbook

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:16.810
where you can post a message and
see it online using cloud data

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:19.030
store as the back end.

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:21.670
And it just exposes
two little APIs

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:24.940
in a very Go-friendly
style of doing that.

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:27.910
And it serves some static
content using the HTTP file

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:30.090
server object.

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:32.090
But this time around I'm
going to pretend that I

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:33.230
don't have Docker installed.

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:35.063
And I'm going to say,
hey, I have this code.

00:12:35.063 --> 00:12:37.010
How do I actually go
about building that code

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:39.020
and having it run
on the service?

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:41.600
So, again, I create a Docker
file, a very simple one that

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:43.470
installs some dependencies.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:46.880
And this time around I am going
to get in and build some YAML.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:49.580
So I'm going to define a
service, like G Cloud did

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:50.762
for me automatically.

00:12:50.762 --> 00:12:52.220
And I'm going to
say that my source

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:55.400
code comes from this
particular Git repository.

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:57.124
And I want to use
the master branch.

00:12:57.124 --> 00:12:59.540
And then I'm going to use the
kaniko build template that's

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:01.370
baked into the
serverless add-on,

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:03.560
and have it build
that code for me,

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:06.740
and push it into this
container registry.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:10.610
And then I want it to deploy
that container into my code.

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:12.370
And so I'm going to
do kubectl apply.

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:19.852
And if I go out and
get my pods, you'll

00:13:19.852 --> 00:13:22.060
see there's this new pod
that isn't a deployment pod.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:24.730
That's actually the
build pod spinning up.

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:28.510
And I'm going to go out and take
a look at Stackdriver, where,

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:30.752
of course, because this is
integratedly Google Cloud,

00:13:30.752 --> 00:13:32.960
Stackdriver is automatically
plumbed through as well.

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:34.610
So I can see what's going on.

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:38.430
And I'm going to search
for build in my logs.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:42.611
Hopefully, I'll
see the right logs.

00:13:42.611 --> 00:13:43.610
Maybe I'm not streaming.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:44.390
That's why.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:44.971
There we go.

00:13:44.971 --> 00:13:45.470
All right.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:48.270
So here's a bunch
of build output.

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:51.100
And you'll see it actually
ended up pushing the container.

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:55.490
Now ignore the fact
that everything shows up

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:56.330
as a warning.

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:58.040
Like I said, this is an alpha.

00:13:58.040 --> 00:13:59.900
There's a few
quirks to work out.

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:01.790
That might be one of them.

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:02.300
All right.

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:04.160
If I go back here and
get my pods again.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:05.030
The build is done.

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:07.010
You'll actually see I
now have a deployment.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:10.910
And I can go to my
guestbook application.

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:12.465
And, if everything
works right, I

00:14:12.465 --> 00:14:14.090
should see some
guestbook entries here.

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:21.580
Oh, that's not what
I wanted to see.

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:23.560
Oh, all right, I see what I--

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:24.430
I jumped the gun.

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:26.450
It wasn't actually running.

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:27.530
Now it's running.

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:30.160
Let's try it again.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:30.834
There we go.

00:14:30.834 --> 00:14:31.750
Now it actually works.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:33.694
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:36.382 --> 00:14:38.090
And just to prove it,
I'll post something

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:39.173
and you'll see it show up.

00:14:39.173 --> 00:14:39.710
Cool.

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:41.522
Now, that's neat.

00:14:41.522 --> 00:14:43.980
Let's see what happens if I
actually throw some load at it.

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:47.720
So, in this window, I'm
going to use watch--

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:49.596
kubectl get pods.

00:14:49.596 --> 00:14:51.470
And you'll see right
now I have a Hello World

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:53.420
pod and a guest book pod.

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:55.940
And I'm going to run
a little script that's

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:58.910
going to throw 10 queries--

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:02.330
or 10 threads and 250
queries at this thing.

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:05.300
And you'll see that the
serverless add-on is responding

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:08.030
to the load by scaling out the
containers that I have here.

00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:10.280
And it's going to make sure
that all of those requests

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:13.000
are satisfied as fast as
possible by spinning up

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:17.864
as much capacity as it
can inside my cluster.

00:15:17.864 --> 00:15:19.355
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:25.336
So that's really exciting.

00:15:25.336 --> 00:15:27.460
The last part of this demo
that I was hoping-- yep,

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:28.180
there it goes.

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:31.330
So you see the Hello World demo
is now actually spinning down.

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:34.450
That pod is terminating
because nobody in the audience

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:35.890
actually went to that URL.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:36.854
And so it's timed out.

00:15:36.854 --> 00:15:38.020
It's hit the time-out limit.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:39.694
And now that pod is
going to go away.

00:15:39.694 --> 00:15:41.860
And so you can see it's
actually saving me resources

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:44.140
inside my pod, or
inside my cluster,

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:46.630
rather, because of that.

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:50.320
So this is just a really
brief introduction to what

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:51.830
the serverless add-on can do.

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:54.180
There's so much
more baked in here.

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:57.050
And we're going to tell you
a little bit more today.

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:59.320
But tomorrow, at
the same time slot,

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:01.480
actually, is a very
deep, technical deep-dive

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:03.190
into the underlying
technology on this

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:04.457
that you should come to.

00:16:04.457 --> 00:16:06.790
And you can learn all about
all of the intricate details

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:08.930
in here.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:09.430
OK.

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:10.971
If we can switch
back to the slides--

00:16:13.785 --> 00:16:15.690
cool.

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:18.020
So just to recap the
demo really quickly--

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.950
we went on and we turned on a
cluster inside Kubernetes that

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:22.455
deployed the serverless add-on.

00:16:22.455 --> 00:16:24.830
So I didn't have to load a
bunch of manifests or anything

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:26.210
to get all this stuff installed.

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:28.850
It was just there by
default inside my cluster--

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:30.860
or there when I enabled
the add-on rather.

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:32.276
We then deployed
a Hello World app

00:16:32.276 --> 00:16:34.310
from a container using
the G Cloud command

00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:35.726
line to make it really easy.

00:16:35.726 --> 00:16:37.100
And then we built
source actually

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:39.570
on the cluster, safely
contained inside the cluster.

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:42.650
So nothing had to leave
my cluster by doing that.

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:45.260
And then we showed how
it scaled up and down.

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:47.419
And then, events is
another big piece of this.

00:16:47.419 --> 00:16:49.460
And, like I said, I didn't
have time in this demo

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:50.400
to show off events.

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:52.790
But come tomorrow and
we'll talk about how events

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:55.100
work inside this space as well.

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:57.410
But it's great
for me from Google

00:16:57.410 --> 00:17:00.305
to stand up here and tell you
how awesome my technology is.

00:17:00.305 --> 00:17:02.180
But you shouldn't just
take my word for this.

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:04.819
So I'd like to invite
Ram from T-Mobile up.

00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:06.646
He's the principal
technology architect

00:17:06.646 --> 00:17:08.270
to tell you a little
bit more about how

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:10.190
T-Mobile has been
using the technology

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:11.442
inside the serverless add-on.

00:17:11.442 --> 00:17:12.150
RAM GOPINATHAN: Thank you, Ryan.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:13.275
RYAN GREGG: Thank you, Ram.

00:17:13.275 --> 00:17:14.108
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:23.079
RAM GOPINATHAN: Thank you Ryan
and DeWitt for having me here.

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:28.089
First of all, huge
congrats on the milestone.

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:31.540
So we're really-- from all of
us at T-Mobile, huge congrats

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:34.000
to you guys.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:39.190
So choice of running the
workloads in different clouds

00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:41.630
is important for us.

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:44.670
So when the Google
team asked us to test

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:48.590
drive the serverless add-on,
we couldn't be more excited.

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:50.980
So what I want to do in
this next couple of slides

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:54.740
is talk about a use case
that we picked to run--

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:56.450
to test drive the
serverless add-on.

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:01.730
So, basically what
you're seeing here

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:06.260
is, we have this experience
called store locator.

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:07.970
Pretty much as you
can guess, this

00:18:07.970 --> 00:18:13.490
is used by our customers to
see what stores are nearby,

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:17.090
so they can call the store
or even get directions

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:21.880
so they can go out to the
store and buy services.

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:26.370
So just to tell you a little
bit about the service that's

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:28.110
in production today--

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:30.360
it's basically a
Java application

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:34.800
that is containerized and
running in our Mesos marathon

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:36.000
container platform.

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:40.950
Not only we had to
build the service,

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:44.340
we also had to write code
to actually collect metrics,

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:48.690
send the metrics to a
time series database.

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:53.610
Scaling is largely manual
because we get alerts

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:58.240
from Grafana and then we create
dashboards to actually monitor.

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:00.000
And then, when there's
alerts kicking in,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.630
we go and manually scale it.

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:07.080
The other part is
it's always running.

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:09.360
Even when the
service is not used

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:12.300
it's always running, which
means it's taking resources

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:14.470
from the cluster.

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:20.550
So, if it's not used, why
take resources, right?

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:23.730
You could use it for other
workloads, as, I think,

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:26.160
Ryan had talked
about previously.

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:28.590
The last bit here is there's
a lot of investment that's

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:32.190
gone into CI/CD to get
proper pipeline wired up,

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:37.370
to go from dev all the
way into production.

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:39.900
So we thought this
was a great use case

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:42.150
because it's not like always--

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:44.265
people are not always using it--

00:19:44.265 --> 00:19:46.520
usually when you have
to go to a store.

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:51.290
So it fits perfectly
for server scenarios.

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:53.030
So what did we do?

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:57.070
We basically ported the
existing code, which

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:58.520
is a big Java application.

00:19:58.520 --> 00:20:01.890
We ported that, just the logic
required-- the business logic--

00:20:01.890 --> 00:20:05.860
into Go lang functions.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:09.010
And, because we were
building it on top

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:11.440
of the serverless
add-on, we didn't really

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:17.380
have to worry about
creating the metrics,

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:21.086
sending the metrics to a time
series database, and whatnot.

00:20:21.086 --> 00:20:22.960
We didn't have to worry
about log collection.

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:27.550
We didn't have to worry
about scaling, auto scaling.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:31.150
And our devs just had to focus
on just bringing the business

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:32.950
logic over.

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:39.690
We finished this project in
literally a two-week sprint,

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:42.240
which was kind of cool.

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:47.780
Getting a little bit deeper
into the architecture here--

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:50.850
so as you can see
from this slide,

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:53.100
we basically took the
business logic code

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:54.910
and split it into two functions.

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:59.100
One is the indexing
piece, indexer function.

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:02.040
What that does is it
takes the store data

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:05.110
and puts it into an
Elasticsearch index.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:09.960
Because the serverless add-on
is built on top of Kubernetes,

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:14.430
we were able to deploy a
production-grade Elasticsearch

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:18.171
cluster into our same
infrastructure with a helm

00:21:18.171 --> 00:21:18.670
chart.

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:20.622
So that's pretty easy.

00:21:20.622 --> 00:21:22.080
And then the other
function you see

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:24.510
here is the query
function, which

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:27.750
takes the request from
the app and executes

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:31.110
a geo-distance search queries
against the Elasticsearch

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:33.330
and shows you the results.

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:35.357
So pretty straightforward.

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:43.250
Now I want to talk a little bit
about where are going with it.

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:45.920
So today we have,
as I said earlier,

00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:49.400
we have store data
in different sources.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:52.510
We want to actually kind
of create, basically,

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:55.000
a BigQuery dataset and
have all of the store

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:57.430
data in a central location.

00:21:57.430 --> 00:22:01.390
And then when the store data
changes, based on those events,

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:05.080
we want the index to be
automatically updated

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:10.680
so that our customers are always
seeing the most current data.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:14.010
And then T-Mobile's
pretty heavy into--

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:15.610
supportive of open source.

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:17.910
So we want to open
source the store data set

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:21.960
so that other people or other
developers in the industry

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:23.550
can actually build
something that we

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:26.310
didn't imagine or experience.

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:28.590
So we want to open
source the data set.

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:30.690
We also want to open
source the function code

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:33.330
because Store
Locator is something

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:35.910
that pretty much
every retailer has,

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:37.480
the same kind of lead, right?

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:40.320
If you look at Best Buy
or different applications,

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:43.020
the Store Locator is
something we see everywhere.

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:44.670
So we want to open
source that as well.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:50.040
And then the last bit
here is we're always

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:52.870
looking for ways to improve the
experience for our customer,

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:53.610
make it easy.

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:56.550
So we want to integrate
with Google Assistant

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:59.820
so that it gives a natural
language kind of interaction

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:01.770
with the experience.

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:03.310
I think that's about it.

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:03.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:05.018
So hopefully this was useful.

00:23:05.018 --> 00:23:07.450
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:12.150
Thanks.

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:13.100
Thanks, Ram.

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:15.710
It's been a pleasure working
with you and the T-Mobile team.

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:17.656
And we really
appreciate your passion

00:23:17.656 --> 00:23:20.030
around the serverless technology
that we're building here

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:21.980
and where we're going
to go in the future.

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:24.438
And I can't wait to see where
you guys take this stuff too.

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:29.860
So that's the GKE
serverless add-on.

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:33.510
Like I said, it's available
in an alpha program right now.

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:35.490
If you would like to
request access to that,

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:37.870
please check out this URL.

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:41.580
It'll be available for
everybody later this year.

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:45.336
But, you know, there's a lot
of time left in this talk.

00:23:45.336 --> 00:23:46.140
Huh.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:48.010
T-Mobile talked a lot
about open source too.

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:49.320
That might be a good idea.

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:50.160
What do you think?

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:51.420
DEWITT CLINTON: Maybe we
should talk a little bit more

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:52.450
about that.

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:53.417
RYAN GREGG: All right.

00:23:53.417 --> 00:23:54.750
DEWITT CLINTON: Thank you, Ryan.

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:56.160
Thank you, Ram.

00:23:56.160 --> 00:24:00.300
So as we were building the
serverless add-on for GKE

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:03.510
and working with
customers like T-Mobile,

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:05.700
we had the opportunity
to survey the space.

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.980
And we realized
that the marriage

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:12.660
of serverless and Kubernetes is
attracting a lot of interest.

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:15.150
And, rather than
shy away from that,

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:17.190
we saw it as a
perfect opportunity

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:19.690
to bring everybody
together to collaborate.

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:22.800
And that's what we're
going to talk about now.

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:25.920
I'm very pleased
to introduce today

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:28.890
Knative, which
provides the building

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:33.647
blocks for running serverless
workloads on Kubernetes.

00:24:33.647 --> 00:24:35.950
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:36.449
Thank you.

00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:42.320
And what I'm going to show
you should look familiar

00:24:42.320 --> 00:24:45.074
because Ryan just showed
it to you in the demo.

00:24:45.074 --> 00:24:46.490
This is real
technology that we're

00:24:46.490 --> 00:24:49.532
deploying into our real
clusters and supporting.

00:24:49.532 --> 00:24:50.990
And we believe
that's the right way

00:24:50.990 --> 00:24:53.870
to approach an
open-source problem.

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:56.480
But you don't do it alone.

00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:59.090
Doing this right
requires an ecosystem.

00:24:59.090 --> 00:25:02.640
And we are very proud to have
worked with some of the biggest

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:07.220
and leading names in
cloud, like Pivotal and SAP

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:12.620
and Red Hat and IBM to codify
the commonalities for running

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:14.550
serverless on Kubernetes.

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:17.340
And we're going to hear
from some of them today.

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:22.850
Before I get started, though, I
want to show you the landscape

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:24.990
and show you where
in the overall stack

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:27.290
we think that Knative fits in.

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:29.920
So we start first
with Kubernetes,

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:31.334
the universal platform.

00:25:31.334 --> 00:25:32.000
It's ubiquitous.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:33.440
It runs everywhere.

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:34.530
We take that for granted.

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:38.390
And that's what allows us
to do this, which is build

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:40.680
a layer of building blocks--

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:44.390
these primitive components--
for essential operations

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:47.240
for running serverless
workloads on top of Kubernetes.

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:51.729
We're starting with Build
and Serving and Eventing.

00:25:51.729 --> 00:25:53.270
But this is just
the beginning and we

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:55.200
expect it to grow from here.

00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:56.690
In fact, I've
heard this referred

00:25:56.690 --> 00:26:00.980
to as the standard library
for running serverless

00:26:00.980 --> 00:26:02.540
on Kubernetes.

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:05.920
And I think I'm OK
with that analogy.

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:07.580
And here we see the
type of products

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:11.210
that we envision being
built on top of Knative.

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:14.480
Google is sharing a few of
those with the world today.

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:17.780
Earlier today we learned about
SAP's Kyma, Pivotal's function

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:18.320
service.

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:19.670
And we'll talk about those now.

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:25.440
Again, I can't emphasize enough,
so I added a whole other slide

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:26.600
just to say it.

00:26:26.600 --> 00:26:29.090
This is not a
science fair project.

00:26:29.090 --> 00:26:32.290
This is not a 20% open source
project that we're shipping.

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:34.150
Rather, it's Google
products that we

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:39.560
are taking the open source
out of and giving away.

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:41.980
And we're not doing it alone.

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:46.690
Earlier today, SAP
announced SAP Kyma,

00:26:46.690 --> 00:26:51.980
which is based on Knative
serverless components.

00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:54.620
They say that
Knative will further

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:58.100
enable SAP to accelerate
enterprise serverless

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:00.540
and event-based applications.

00:27:00.540 --> 00:27:03.440
SAP has been an incredible
development partner throughout

00:27:03.440 --> 00:27:06.310
and I'm really excited about
what's going on with Kyma.

00:27:06.310 --> 00:27:08.595
They're around this
week at the SAP booth

00:27:08.595 --> 00:27:10.220
and I hope that
everybody gets a chance

00:27:10.220 --> 00:27:14.300
to stop by and see what
we're doing with Kyma.

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:19.070
And IBM cloud is
investing in Knative.

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:22.010
IBM, of course, has extensive
background and experience

00:27:22.010 --> 00:27:25.630
running serverless workloads
at scale with OpenWhisk.

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:27.620
And we are working
together, and they

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:31.460
are leading, and bringing
their leadership and expertise

00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:35.000
to help bring serverless to a
wider range of applications,

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:36.935
developers, and industry
than ever before.

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:43.690
And Red Hat, who's been focused
on open source and customer

00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:47.620
choice since the beginning,
is investing in Knative

00:27:47.620 --> 00:27:50.440
to create a common
building block

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:52.210
for serverless on
top of Kubernetes

00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:54.010
across the hybrid cloud.

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:56.110
Absolutely wonderful
working with Red Hat.

00:27:56.110 --> 00:27:59.210
Very excited to see this in the
open shift function service.

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:04.820
And T-Mobile isn't
just a customer.

00:28:04.820 --> 00:28:07.700
T-Mobile is also a
contributor to Knative.

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:11.420
With their Jazz framework
they are integrating Knative

00:28:11.420 --> 00:28:14.450
into the very fabric
that T-Mobile runs on.

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:17.960
Knative and Kubernetes and
Jazz are working beautifully

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:21.110
together to accelerate
T-Mobile's move

00:28:21.110 --> 00:28:23.420
to cloud-native applications.

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:26.870
Been fantastic working with
a partner and a customer who

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:30.095
has so much to add in the
open-source world as well.

00:28:30.095 --> 00:28:30.720
Thank you, Ram.

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:35.770
In fact, this is the beginning.

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:38.030
Maybe the future
of serverless is

00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:41.160
going to be built on
Kubernetes and Knative.

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:45.550
Some of you know Sebastien,
creator of Kubeless.

00:28:45.550 --> 00:28:47.680
He says that Knative
is standardizing

00:28:47.680 --> 00:28:50.680
the primitives needed
to simplify code

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:52.930
to production workflows.

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:55.690
It promises to be
a solid foundation

00:28:55.690 --> 00:28:58.540
to develop the future
of serverless computing.

00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:00.220
It's been amazing
having Seb help out.

00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:00.800
So thank you.

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:05.740
And you've heard me talk a lot.

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:07.960
Now I would like to
bring up my friend Ryan

00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:10.600
Morgan, Vice President of
Application Platform at Pivotal

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:15.550
Software, who's done more
for Knative than anybody.

00:29:15.550 --> 00:29:16.555
Thank you, Ryan.

00:29:16.555 --> 00:29:18.495
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:21.289 --> 00:29:22.580
RYAN MORGAN: Thank you, DeWitt.

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:26.240
It's been fantastic working
with you and the team.

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:27.830
And I'm excited
to share with you

00:29:27.830 --> 00:29:30.520
our experience around Knative
and where we see it going.

00:29:33.630 --> 00:29:35.040
But, first, why serverless?

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:37.560
I think, judging by the
number of hands I saw early,

00:29:37.560 --> 00:29:39.360
maybe I don't need
to explain this.

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:41.970
But at Pivotal our mission
is to transform the way

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:43.790
the world builds software.

00:29:43.790 --> 00:29:46.540
Part of that is being able
to deliver the right compute

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:52.110
abstractions on the cloud of
your choice for your workload.

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:55.252
The top thing on
there, for functions--

00:29:55.252 --> 00:30:00.561
we view that as a really
important enterprise use case.

00:30:00.561 --> 00:30:03.060
And it's an area that we've
been invested in fairly heavily.

00:30:03.060 --> 00:30:05.220
And that's a problem that we
want to solve for our customers

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:05.720
today.

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:09.760
Then why Knative?

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:11.410
About a year ago,
Pivotal partnered

00:30:11.410 --> 00:30:15.220
with VMware and Google to
bring Pivotal container service

00:30:15.220 --> 00:30:16.210
to market.

00:30:16.210 --> 00:30:21.190
Pivotal container service was
a enterprise-grade Kubernetes

00:30:21.190 --> 00:30:24.820
distribution that's
deployable to vSphere

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:29.167
and has the promise of constant
compatibility with GKE.

00:30:29.167 --> 00:30:30.750
We view Kubernetes
as a great platform

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:32.110
for building platforms.

00:30:32.110 --> 00:30:34.186
And, from that, we
started Project Riff.

00:30:34.186 --> 00:30:38.010
Project Riff had the
top goals of being

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:42.600
k8s native with a focus on
event streaming and first class

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:45.510
support for Spring
and for Java, but also

00:30:45.510 --> 00:30:48.310
for other languages as well.

00:30:48.310 --> 00:30:50.330
One thing we've seen
is when you reduce

00:30:50.330 --> 00:30:52.800
the scope of concern
for developers,

00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:54.544
it allows them to
be more productive.

00:30:54.544 --> 00:30:56.210
As the stewards of
the Spring framework,

00:30:56.210 --> 00:30:58.790
we've seen this with Spring
Boot and Functions just

00:30:58.790 --> 00:31:00.840
takes that one step further.

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:02.590
But as we were developing
Riff, we quickly

00:31:02.590 --> 00:31:04.840
realized that there are
some missing pieces.

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:07.120
There's a lot about
container builds,

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:09.850
things about incremental
deploys, auto scaling,

00:31:09.850 --> 00:31:11.380
and things that we
weren't concerned

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:13.480
with at the beginning.

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:15.730
And, at this point,
we were introduced

00:31:15.730 --> 00:31:19.510
to DeWitt and his team
working on Knative.

00:31:19.510 --> 00:31:22.510
And we realized that
Riff and Knative

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:26.110
were very complimentary, that
things that we were working on

00:31:26.110 --> 00:31:29.950
really fit in well with the
plans they had for Knative.

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:34.810
So we began working with Google
to contribute some of our code

00:31:34.810 --> 00:31:36.910
into the Knative project.

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:43.480
And through that we then rebased
the Riff developer experience

00:31:43.480 --> 00:31:48.050
for event-driven
functions onto Knative.

00:31:48.050 --> 00:31:52.440
So, really, we're betting
our future on Knative.

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:55.040
And part of that is our
first release today.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:58.490
So this morning at 9:30, we
released our first version

00:31:58.490 --> 00:32:02.090
of Riff, that is fully
built on top of Knative.

00:32:05.640 --> 00:32:08.270
I also wanted to go into
a few of the contributions

00:32:08.270 --> 00:32:09.630
that we've made to date.

00:32:09.630 --> 00:32:13.190
The first is around the Riff
build, the build templates that

00:32:13.190 --> 00:32:15.540
are provided within Knative.

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:17.570
Through our experience
in Cloud Foundry,

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:19.820
we have a lot of experience
with build packs.

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:23.850
Build packs offer the ability
to just push your code

00:32:23.850 --> 00:32:26.625
and from that generate an
image that's then runnable.

00:32:26.625 --> 00:32:29.056
Build packs are
very easy to write

00:32:29.056 --> 00:32:30.680
and there's been
quite a bit of success

00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:32.740
with build packs
in the FaaS world,

00:32:32.740 --> 00:32:34.160
and we're very
excited to see what

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:37.580
that might bring to Knative.

00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:41.690
The second is around Riff and
the topics and the eventing

00:32:41.690 --> 00:32:46.100
that we were working on there
and the contribution of that

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:49.310
into the channel
abstraction within Knative.

00:32:49.310 --> 00:32:52.910
What that does is it provides
not only functions but also

00:32:52.910 --> 00:32:57.380
applications with the ability
to publish and receive

00:32:57.380 --> 00:33:01.959
streams of events for
event-based functions.

00:33:01.959 --> 00:33:03.500
If you follow Pivotal,
you might hear

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:05.610
us talk about the value line.

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:08.300
The value line is the
point in your architecture

00:33:08.300 --> 00:33:11.000
where changes below that
line are not delivering value

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:14.330
to your end customers.

00:33:14.330 --> 00:33:18.210
We feel that that concept
applies in open source as well.

00:33:18.210 --> 00:33:20.120
And, in this case,
the building blocks

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:22.280
that Knative provide
on top of Kubernetes

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:25.760
allows you as developers to
worry less about infrastructure

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:29.090
and focus more on your code.

00:33:29.090 --> 00:33:31.070
So for Pivotal this all means--

00:33:31.070 --> 00:33:33.880
this really comes down to a new
product called Pivotal Function

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:35.430
Service.

00:33:35.430 --> 00:33:38.190
This will be deployable and
runnable on Pivotal Container

00:33:38.190 --> 00:33:41.910
Service and will provide a
common Function abstraction

00:33:41.910 --> 00:33:45.470
across any cloud or on premise.

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:47.780
It will have strong support
for Java and for Spring,

00:33:47.780 --> 00:33:50.900
but also will support
other languages as well.

00:33:50.900 --> 00:33:52.400
And, like all Pivotal
products, will

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:54.850
have a strong focus on Day 2.

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:56.350
And, most importantly,
these are all

00:33:56.350 --> 00:34:00.250
built upon components that
are built within Knative.

00:34:00.250 --> 00:34:01.607
So this is all very new.

00:34:01.607 --> 00:34:02.690
This is obviously day one.

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:04.690
But we're very excited
about the future of Knative

00:34:04.690 --> 00:34:07.160
and look forward to working
with you all in the community.

00:34:07.160 --> 00:34:07.812
Thank you.

00:34:07.812 --> 00:34:10.764
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:13.397 --> 00:34:14.730
DEWITT CLINTON: Thank you, Ryan.

00:34:14.730 --> 00:34:15.300
Thank you.

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:17.610
I can honestly say
that we would not

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:21.420
be here without the help and
support and contributions

00:34:21.420 --> 00:34:21.929
of Pivotal.

00:34:21.929 --> 00:34:24.480
It's been fantastic working
with Ryan and the team.

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:26.985
I'm really excited about
the future for Riff.

00:34:26.985 --> 00:34:29.219
I'm excited about the
future for Knative

00:34:29.219 --> 00:34:32.130
based on the things we've
learned in that collaboration.

00:34:32.130 --> 00:34:34.139
Really been fantastic.

00:34:34.139 --> 00:34:36.790
So thank you, Ryan.

00:34:36.790 --> 00:34:39.770
Now I'm going to tell
you, at very high level--

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:41.320
so this is very low fidelity--

00:34:41.320 --> 00:34:44.860
a little bit about what
is in Knative today.

00:34:44.860 --> 00:34:48.566
This is early on and we're
just getting started.

00:34:48.566 --> 00:34:50.440
And I'm not going to go
into a lot of detail,

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:53.090
because tomorrow there's
a technical talk, DEV209,

00:34:53.090 --> 00:34:57.580
where the tech leads will go and
unpack this a little bit more.

00:34:57.580 --> 00:35:01.720
So right now it focuses
on the common components

00:35:01.720 --> 00:35:04.900
that everybody needs,
below the value line,

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:08.740
where you want to codify the
commonalities, where you're not

00:35:08.740 --> 00:35:10.700
deriving proprietary
business logic,

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:12.700
but rather something
that's better off if we all

00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:13.611
get it right.

00:35:13.611 --> 00:35:14.110
OK?

00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:19.890
So right now we're starting with
Build and Serving and Events.

00:35:19.890 --> 00:35:21.950
So what does Build provide?

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:25.650
As you know, containers
are the lingua franca--

00:35:25.650 --> 00:35:28.230
the common language--
of Kubernetes.

00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:32.730
And they're the common
language of Knative as well.

00:35:32.730 --> 00:35:36.990
But if you're a developer like
me, you don't write containers.

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:38.154
You write code.

00:35:38.154 --> 00:35:39.570
So you need something
that's going

00:35:39.570 --> 00:35:42.240
to allow you to orchestrate
the process of going

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:44.070
from code to container.

00:35:44.070 --> 00:35:46.050
And what Knative
build primitives

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:50.100
do is allow you to describe that
in such a way that maybe safely

00:35:50.100 --> 00:35:53.100
on cluster with Conoco, or in
the cloud with Google Cloud

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:56.790
build, you can have your
Kubernetes work flow pull

00:35:56.790 --> 00:35:59.331
that code into a container.

00:35:59.331 --> 00:36:01.580
But when you have a container,
what do you do with it?

00:36:01.580 --> 00:36:03.040
You want to serve it.

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:07.690
So what Knative
serving components do

00:36:07.690 --> 00:36:10.270
is allow you to describe
your workload in a way that

00:36:10.270 --> 00:36:11.950
makes sense for the developer.

00:36:11.950 --> 00:36:14.230
You saw some of these
in Ryan's demo--

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:16.480
the route and the
configuration, the sort

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:18.670
of 12-factor app
style of development

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:22.270
that we've learned building
serverless products at Google.

00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:25.720
And that will allow the
system to do things for you,

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:29.710
like scale it from 0
to 1, or 1 to 100,000,

00:36:29.710 --> 00:36:32.865
or maybe even better,
100,000 back down to 0 again.

00:36:32.865 --> 00:36:34.240
So that's the type
of things that

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:37.300
having these standard
primitives allow you to do.

00:36:37.300 --> 00:36:38.020
And that's great.

00:36:38.020 --> 00:36:40.790
Makes a wonderful
day one experience.

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:42.670
But what about day
two, day five, day 100,

00:36:42.670 --> 00:36:44.830
when you're still trying
to run these workloads

00:36:44.830 --> 00:36:46.031
in real production?

00:36:46.031 --> 00:36:47.530
Then you need
something that's smart

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:51.034
about blue-green deployments
and traffic shifting.

00:36:51.034 --> 00:36:52.450
And those are the
other components

00:36:52.450 --> 00:36:56.750
that are inside native serving.

00:36:56.750 --> 00:37:00.080
And you might want to serve
these over HTTP or HTTPS.

00:37:00.080 --> 00:37:00.680
That's great.

00:37:00.680 --> 00:37:01.670
You can do that--

00:37:01.670 --> 00:37:04.580
set up an ingress
rule, set up a route.

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:08.930
But you may also want to attach
it to a larger event ecosystem.

00:37:08.930 --> 00:37:13.640
You may want to attach it to a
Git commit or something being

00:37:13.640 --> 00:37:15.800
flowed off your CI/CD pipeline.

00:37:15.800 --> 00:37:18.650
And what the Knative
eventing primitives do

00:37:18.650 --> 00:37:22.430
is allow you to describe,
in a developer-friendly way,

00:37:22.430 --> 00:37:25.580
the binding from event
producer to event consumer

00:37:25.580 --> 00:37:28.250
and provide the
underlying infrastructure

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:29.140
to make that happen.

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:36.879
So I showed you
what's in the box.

00:37:36.879 --> 00:37:39.170
Let's talk a little bit about
why we made those choices

00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:40.860
and how it got in the box.

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:42.260
So there are some
core principles

00:37:42.260 --> 00:37:44.330
that the development team uses.

00:37:44.330 --> 00:37:48.120
The first is, it
should be idiomatic.

00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:50.290
And by that I mean--

00:37:50.290 --> 00:37:52.170
let's take, for
example, Kubernetes.

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:54.460
If Kubernetes has a
way of doing things,

00:37:54.460 --> 00:37:58.560
if it has a standard, native
way, we'll do it that way.

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:00.530
We won't reinvent the
wheel with Knative.

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:02.170
It should layer
naturally on top.

00:38:02.170 --> 00:38:05.850
You saw the kube-cuddle demo,
where the exact same command

00:38:05.850 --> 00:38:08.700
line works against
Knative as well.

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:14.600
And, also, we shouldn't have you
get a PhD in Knative to use it.

00:38:14.600 --> 00:38:16.280
We should meet
you where you are.

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:19.210
If you're a node developer,
who writes Docker files,

00:38:19.210 --> 00:38:22.480
we should be able to take your
existing idiomatic node code,

00:38:22.480 --> 00:38:24.370
and your existing
idiomatic Docker files,

00:38:24.370 --> 00:38:27.010
and run them and serve them
with all the advantages

00:38:27.010 --> 00:38:28.740
that you would get.

00:38:28.740 --> 00:38:31.160
Second, it should be extensible.

00:38:31.160 --> 00:38:33.590
I showed you these layers
of loose coupling--

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:36.360
Build and Serving and Events.

00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:39.096
And you should be able
to, in your products,

00:38:39.096 --> 00:38:40.220
pick and choose among them.

00:38:40.220 --> 00:38:41.160
You may want Build.

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:42.920
You may want all three.

00:38:42.920 --> 00:38:46.240
But it also should be
pluggable at the bottom.

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:48.660
We saw a demo where it was
wired into Stackdriver.

00:38:48.660 --> 00:38:51.160
You should be able to pull that
out and put in an Oak stack,

00:38:51.160 --> 00:38:52.220
do whatever you need.

00:38:52.220 --> 00:38:54.020
So we should make it
extensible at the top

00:38:54.020 --> 00:38:56.300
but pluggable at the bottom.

00:38:56.300 --> 00:38:59.120
And then, lastly-- and I sort
of struggled to find the right

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:01.020
word for this--

00:39:01.020 --> 00:39:02.510
but it should feel organic.

00:39:02.510 --> 00:39:05.870
And by that I really mean we
should start from the bottoms

00:39:05.870 --> 00:39:10.010
up and codify the commonalities,
the stuff that we all

00:39:10.010 --> 00:39:11.870
agree on that need to be there.

00:39:11.870 --> 00:39:15.860
We shouldn't be inventing
new things that feel rough.

00:39:15.860 --> 00:39:17.780
We should do the things
that you want in order

00:39:17.780 --> 00:39:21.350
to have a stable platform for
the developers in your org.

00:39:21.350 --> 00:39:23.750
So let's talk about
those developers.

00:39:23.750 --> 00:39:27.380
Many of us in this
room are that persona.

00:39:27.380 --> 00:39:29.160
We write code all day.

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:31.490
And that's what we should
be doing for our businesses.

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:34.660
These APIs are designed
for developers.

00:39:34.660 --> 00:39:36.890
They're designed to make
a developer's life easier.

00:39:36.890 --> 00:39:41.060
They are designed to provide
a serverless experience

00:39:41.060 --> 00:39:44.000
on top of Kubernetes.

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:46.760
But how do they get that system?

00:39:46.760 --> 00:39:47.580
Operators.

00:39:47.580 --> 00:39:50.340
Operators provide these
systems to developers.

00:39:50.340 --> 00:39:53.270
In fact, you met an
operator earlier today--

00:39:53.270 --> 00:39:57.980
Ryan-- who's operating the
serverless add-on for GKE.

00:39:57.980 --> 00:39:59.720
And your organization
may have teams

00:39:59.720 --> 00:40:01.770
of operators who are
deploying these things

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:05.330
for your businesses.

00:40:05.330 --> 00:40:06.680
And maybe all of us--

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:08.390
hopefully all of us--

00:40:08.390 --> 00:40:10.790
will also be contributors,
that this is something

00:40:10.790 --> 00:40:13.375
that we all want to work on
together to get it right.

00:40:13.375 --> 00:40:15.500
And we strongly believe in
the power of open source

00:40:15.500 --> 00:40:15.999
to do this.

00:40:19.490 --> 00:40:21.830
So why is Google
investing in it?

00:40:21.830 --> 00:40:23.370
Why are we doing it?

00:40:23.370 --> 00:40:25.520
It's clearly not easy.

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:29.650
Frankly, we're doing it
because you asked us to.

00:40:29.650 --> 00:40:32.230
You're moving your
businesses to Kubernetes.

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:34.330
But you don't want to
leave serverless behind.

00:40:34.330 --> 00:40:36.350
You want to bring
serverless with you.

00:40:36.350 --> 00:40:38.320
So Google is responding
by helping out

00:40:38.320 --> 00:40:42.370
by building serverless
on top of Kubernetes.

00:40:42.370 --> 00:40:46.610
Then you asked, why are we
doing it in open source?

00:40:46.610 --> 00:40:50.020
Well, you also want
workload portability.

00:40:50.020 --> 00:40:53.410
You want the freedom of choice
to run wherever you want,

00:40:53.410 --> 00:40:56.122
in your data center or in ours.

00:40:56.122 --> 00:40:58.330
And we want to help provide
that through open source.

00:41:01.790 --> 00:41:04.202
So, to recap--

00:41:04.202 --> 00:41:07.920
Knative is providing
those building blocks

00:41:07.920 --> 00:41:11.220
for running serverless
on Kubernetes.

00:41:11.220 --> 00:41:14.749
And today we're starting with
Build and Serving and Events.

00:41:14.749 --> 00:41:16.290
It's very early days
and we expect it

00:41:16.290 --> 00:41:19.110
to grow rather big from here.

00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:22.480
And we're doing this so you can
run wherever you want to run.

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:23.940
Wherever you can
run Kubernetes, we

00:41:23.940 --> 00:41:27.310
think you should be able to
run your serverless workloads.

00:41:27.310 --> 00:41:31.210
And we're very pleased to be
doing this, not on our own,

00:41:31.210 --> 00:41:33.775
but with a very broad
community of industry partners.

00:41:36.390 --> 00:41:38.700
And, best of all-- and I've
been waiting to say this

00:41:38.700 --> 00:41:40.260
for a really long time--

00:41:40.260 --> 00:41:41.640
Knative is open today.

00:41:41.640 --> 00:41:42.652
You can go to GitHub.

00:41:42.652 --> 00:41:44.360
I put this slide last
so you wouldn't all

00:41:44.360 --> 00:41:45.693
go at the beginning of the talk.

00:41:45.693 --> 00:41:49.170
But go to GitHub.com/knative
and get involved.

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:51.040
We're really excited
to open it today.

00:41:51.040 --> 00:41:52.464
So thank you.

00:41:52.464 --> 00:41:54.924
